# Turns, in a light aeroplane: rolling in, holding the turn, rolling out.
#
#   name TEXT...                   what the lesson is called
#   teaches CLASS                  which class of aircraft it is for
#   stage TEXT...                  a stage, in the order flown
#   do TEXT...                     what to do at this stage
#   until PROPERTY <=|>= VALUE     what ends the stage
#   hold PROPERTY LOW HIGH TEXT... a band that must hold for the whole stage
#   need PROPERTY <=|>= VALUE TEXT...  what must be true by the stage's end
#
# with `#` beginning a comment; src/sim/lesson.hpp owns the format.
#
# **The height is held against where the stage began**, not against a number.
# A turn is flown anywhere; what the lesson teaches is that the aeroplane
# comes out of it at the height it went in at. `start` is what the property
# read when the stage began, so `start-200 start+200` is "within two hundred
# feet of where you were" wherever that was.
#
# **This is a left turn**, because a bank angle has a sign and a lesson has to
# pick one. The right-hand version is the same lesson with the signs turned
# over, and is not written until something needs it.
#
# **`lesson/turned-deg` is how far she has turned since the stage began**, and
# it is the runner own, not the aeroplane. An earlier draft ended the turn on
# `attitude/psi-deg <= start-60`, which works only while the turn does not
# pass north: a heading of 340 is not less than 70 less sixty, and the stage
# would never end. Turning is a difference, and a raw heading cannot express
# one.
#
# The skill being taught is the height, not the bank: a turn that loses a
# couple of hundred feet is the fault this lesson exists to name.
#
# **The height band is measured.** Flown by the autopilot through ninety
# degrees of left turn at about twenty-six degrees of bank, the four light
# aircraft hold their height to within ten feet of where they started - the
# 172 to 2,990 of 3,000, the 182 to 2,991, the PA-28 to 2,996, the Cub to
# 3,003. A hundred and fifty feet is therefore fifteen times the error a
# correctly flown turn actually makes, and is close to the hundred feet a
# licence test allows.
#
# **A first draft allowed two hundred feet and caught nothing.** The turn
# stage lasts about eleven seconds at this bank and speed, so an aeroplane let
# sink at eight hundred feet a minute through it loses about a hundred and
# fifty - inside the band. A band has to be set against how long the stage
# lasts, not against how bad the flying feels.

name Turns in a light aeroplane
teaches light-aircraft

stage Rolling into the turn
do Look out, then roll smoothly into about thirty degrees of bank to the left
do Hold the nose up as the bank comes on: a turn needs more lift, not less
until attitude/phi-deg <= -20

stage Holding the turn
do Hold the bank steady and the nose where it was
do Keep the height: this is what the turn is for
hold attitude/phi-deg -45.0 -12.0 Hold the bank steady through the turn
hold position/h-agl-ft start-150 start+150 Hold your height through the turn
until lesson/turned-deg <= -60

stage Rolling out
do Roll the wings level and let the nose settle
until attitude/phi-deg >= -5
